            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, and the weather stripping along the exterior doors. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

A few of the windows do not appear to close properly (such as the front right bedroom window close to the main door). As well as some leakage observed around the window frames. A common practice was to use fiberglass batts to fill gaps around windows. I recommend to examine one of these by removing the trim. If fiberglass batts are found 'stuffed' into the gaps, I recommend removing these and replacing with low expansion sprayfoam. 

There was quite a bit of air leakage observed coming from the crawlspace into the furnace room/mechanical room. There is an opening through the wall close to the top of the stairs, which you can view into the floor joist (and the water lines are). This air leakage is likely being introduced through the connecting wall of the mechanical room and crawlspace (small rectangled bump out within the crawlspace). This wall appeared to be well insualted, however poorly air sealed, allowing cold air to easily infiltrate through into the mechnical room. I recommend to investigate potentially sprayfoam this 'bump out' wall in the crawlspace. Sprayfoam, when a minimum of 2 inches thick, will act as both a vapour and air barrier. 

Other penetrations within the mechnical room area, including wire penetrations around the electrical box and light fixture by the water heater. I recommend sealing these penetrations using caulking, or low expension sprayfoam for larger openings.  

Combustin Air Supply: A 6 inch combustion air supply pipe was observed, which is designed to ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your furnace are satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the furnace is operating. 

Air leakage was also observed through both of the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 240 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 9.9 GJ.

I suspect by tackling the adjoining crawlspace/mechnical wall, a 20% improvement in air leakage would be achievable. </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with no insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home. Generally, upgrading wall insulation is an expensive upgrade and only a good consideration for when a siding upgrade is already planned. 



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 420 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 40.0 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your closed crawlspace walls appear to be 8 inches of conrete with no insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of your crawlspace walls.



Savings:

By adding 4 inches (R20) extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ 530 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 430 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space exterior wall area, including the header area, and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

All of your windows are triple pane that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, they are however in reasonable shape. As your home has quite a large glazing ratio (window to wall ratio) I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of your windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 

Similar to a wall upgrade, windows tend to carry a large price tag, which creates a longer payback period. I recommend considering replacing windows that are not functioning/closing properly (if they are not repairable. 



Savings: 

By replacing all of your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 490 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.9 GJ.  



</windows>
                <doors>

You mentioned considering replacing your front/back doors as they appear to be slightly warped and does not create a tight seal when closed, I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified door rated for climate zone 3.



Savings:

By replacing your front and back door with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified door rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 10 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Although the savings appear quite minimal, this only considers of the added insulation value of the new doors. The air leakage, which is the main concern is considered under the air sealing above. </doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a large 287  litre electric hot water tank, which is quite big for residential applications. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $ 1,200 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 0.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil-fired furnace was originally rated at 80.5% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. High efficiency models are sealed combustion, therefore this upgrade will also allow for the elimination of the tall flue of the current furnace, as well as the combustion air supply pipe. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 49.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------







LED LIGHTS ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



VENTILATION

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



WOOD STORAGE

There was mention that the small enclosed wood storage area was generating a lot of moisture (and frost) which ultimately rotted the decking above. This moisture accumulation is likely due to the wood drying out, as well as weather related humidity. I recommend to add two vents/openings into this area, one up high and another low, creating passive ventilation into this area, This passiveventilation will allow the humidy to excape as it naturally rises, and avoid condensing / being trapped on the underside of the above decking. 

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61 per litre

Wood - $420 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

